#include <stdio.h>

void squares(int n) {
    int sq[n];         // variable-length array: n is known only when the program runs
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sq[i] = i * i;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", sq[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void) {
    squares(3);
    squares(6);
    return 0;
}

blocks.text:
Each call to `squares` makes a fresh array of the size it needs. The course notebooks use VLAs the same way, for example `char dest1[len + 1];` where `len` was computed by `strlen`. Rules for a VLA:

- it must be a local variable (declared inside a function);
- it cannot have an initialiser list (`int sq[n] = {0};` is an error); fill it with a loop instead;
- its size must be greater than 0 (a size of 0 or less is undefined behaviour), and should be small (it lives on the *stack*, which you will meet later).

