### Building a NEW string, character by character

To make a string that did not exist before:

1. work out its length `n`;
2. declare a `char` array of size `n + 1` (a variable-length array if `n` is computed at run time);
3. fill indexes `0` to `n - 1` with a loop;
4. put `'\0'` at index `n`.

Only then is it a string that `printf("%s\n", s)`, `puts(s)` and `strlen(s)` can use. Here `stars(n)` builds a string of `n` stars:

blocks.code:
#include <stdio.h>

void stars(int n) {
    char s[n + 1];             // n stars + 1 for '\0'
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        s[i] = '*';
    }
    s[n] = '\0';               // end the string
    printf("[%s]\n", s);
}

int main(void) {
    stars(3);
    stars(7);
    stars(0);
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char src[] = "hello";
    size_t n = strlen(src);    // 5
    char dest[n + 1];          // 6 chars: room for '\0'

    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        dest[i] = src[i];
    }
    dest[n] = '\0';

    dest[0] = 'J';             // dest is a separate copy
    printf("%s\n", src);
    puts(dest);
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    printf("argc = %d\n", argc);
    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        printf("argv[%d] = \"%s\"\n", i, argv[i]);
    }
    return 0;
}

blocks.text:
So `argc` is always at least 1, and with no arguments `argc` is 1 (in Bash, `$#` would be 0).

### Check argc first

If the user gave fewer arguments than you expect, `argv[1]` or `argv[2]` is not a string you may use. Reading it is undefined behaviour (often a crash). So test `argc` **before** using `argv[i]`.

A good program prints a usage message to **standard error** and exits with a non-zero status, like a careful Bash script. `fprintf(stderr, ...)` works exactly like `printf`, but writes to standard error instead of standard output:

```c
if (argc != 2) {
    fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s word\n", argv[0]);
    return 1;
}
```

### argv[i][j] is a char

`argv[i]` is a string, so `argv[i][j]` is its character at index `j`, a `char` (`%c`). Each `argv[i]` ends with `'\0'`, so `strlen(argv[i])` works on it:

blocks.code:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s word\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }
    size_t n = strlen(argv[1]);
    for (size_t j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        printf("argv[1][%zu] = '%c'\n", j, argv[1][j]);
    }
    return 0;
}

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // 1. check argc before touching argv[1]
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "usage: %s word\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }
    // 2. measure the input
    size_t n = strlen(argv[1]);
    // 3. make an array big enough: 2 copies of each char, plus '\0'
    char d[2 * n + 1];
    // 4. copy characters into their new positions
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        d[2 * i] = argv[1][i];
        d[2 * i + 1] = argv[1][i];
    }
    // 5. terminate
    d[2 * n] = '\0';
    // 6. print the NEW string
    printf("%s\n", d);
    return 0;
}

